 @Taijifufu  It's implict in how current oleds work, so all have this problem. Thing is, it also depends a lot in the game; some make it flicker like crazy, some don't, so using an oled with VRR enabled is like going through a dense minefield. But all flicker with fps swings and loading screens.
Keeping framerate consistent works in some games, not in others, and it sounds easier than it actually is, since it means ABSOLUTELY consistent fps.
 If theres a dip in fps big enough for even an amount of time shorter than the time between typical overlay / benchmark tools sample rate pauses, it will flicker, even if the overlay chart says that there was no dip at all.

 @victorro8760 Pulsar also doesn't work with HDR...
I would describe Pulsar as a specialized tool for specific use cases, but OLED is better for wide variety of use cases.
So unless you're a competitive multiplayer or a esports athlete Pulsar will be as niche as some other Nvidia led technologies have been.
